# Node server discipline

Match the project's existing framework, routing, and error-handling patterns before
adding handlers or middleware.

Validate and sanitize external input at route boundaries. Do not trust query params,
headers, or request bodies without the same checks the codebase already uses.

Handle async errors explicitly — use existing error middleware or try/catch in
handlers so rejected promises do not crash the process silently.

Avoid blocking the event loop with synchronous CPU-heavy work or large sync I/O in
request paths. Prefer existing job queues or background workers when the repo
already uses them.

Do not introduce new HTTP clients, ORMs, or auth libraries without evidence the
project lacks a suitable existing dependency.
